            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Lists the positions of every true (non-zero) cell in an array.\n\nWhere np.where uses a mask to build a new array, np.argwhere tells you WHERE the mask is set: which joints are over the speed limit, which frames are glitched, which pixels are above threshold. Each found cell comes out as its coordinates.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.argwhere\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nINPUTS:\n\ncondition:\nAny array. A cell counts as found when it is true, or non-zero for numbers - so a comparison's result is the usual input, but a plain numeric array works too. Sends on arrival.\n\n(np.argwhere is built by the same code as np.where but has only this one inlet: it does not take 'if true' or 'if false'.)\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nindices:\nAn integer array with one ROW per found cell and one column per dimension of the input: for a 2-D mask each row is (row, column). The rows come in reading order - along the first row, then the next. Its shape is (number found, number of dimensions); when nothing is found it is empty, shape (0, 2) for a 2-D mask.\n\nFeed one of those rows to np.[] (or split the columns with np.[] :,0 and :,1) to go from where to what.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nnp.rand makes an 8 x 8 array of noise, once when the page opens and again at each click of the button; > 0.9 keeps roughly one cell in ten. The heat_map shows that mask in two tones - white for found, black for not. Under it, np.argwhere lists the found cells as [row, column] pairs, and np.shape counts them. Check a pair against the picture: row 0 is the top, column 0 the left. Drag 0.9 on the > node to change how many are found (it applies from the next click).\n\nRELATED:\nnp.where uses the same mask to choose values. The comparison nodes (>, <, ...) make the mask. t.argwhere is the PyTorch version (on the t.any page). np.[] takes values out once you know where they are.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Lists the positions of every true (non-zero) cell in an array.\n\nWhere np.where uses a mask to build a new array, np.argwhere tells you WHERE the mask is set: which joints are over the speed limit, which frames are glitched, which pixels are above threshold. Each found cell comes out as its coordinates.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.argwhere\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nINPUTS:\n\ncondition:\nAny array. A cell counts as found when it is true, or non-zero for numbers - so a comparison's result is the usual input, but a plain numeric array works too. Sends on arrival.\n\n(np.argwhere is built by the same code as np.where but has only this one inlet: it does not take 'if true' or 'if false'.)\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nindices:\nAn integer array with one ROW per found cell and one column per dimension of the input: for a 2-D mask each row is (row, column). The rows come in reading order - along the first row, then the next. Its shape is (number found, number of dimensions); when nothing is found it is empty, shape (0, 2) for a 2-D mask.\n\nFeed one of those rows to np.[] (or split the columns with np.[] :,0 and :,1) to go from where to what.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nnp.rand makes an 8 x 8 array of noise, once when the page opens and again at each click of the button; > 0.9 keeps roughly one cell in ten. The heat_map shows that mask in two tones - white for found, black for not. Under it, np.argwhere lists the found cells as [row, column] pairs, and np.shape counts them. Check a pair against the picture: row 0 is the top, column 0 the left. Drag 0.9 on the > node to change how many are found (it applies from the next click).\n\nRELATED:\nnp.where uses the same mask to choose values. The comparison nodes (>, <, ...) make the mask. t.argwhere is the PyTorch version (on the t.any page). np.[] takes values out once you know where they are."
        },
